(# Crypto: Reused Nonce \(AES-CTR\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Summary) Tj
T*
(Recover the flag by exploiting AES-CTR nonce reuse, which reuses the same keystream for every) Tj
T*
(encryption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Challenge) Tj
T*
(- The server encrypts user-provided plaintext via POST /encrypt.) Tj
T*
(- It also returns a target ciphertext via GET /target \(the flag encrypted\).) Tj
T*
(- Vulnerability: AES-CTR is used with a fixed nonce \(FIXED_NONCE\) for all requests.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Solution) Tj
T*
(In CTR mode:) Tj
T*
(- C = P XOR keystream) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(If the same nonce is reused, the keystream repeats. If we can ask the server to encrypt a known) Tj
T*
(plaintext \(like all-zero bytes\), we directly learn the keystream:) Tj
T*
(- encrypt\(00..00\) = keystream) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Then decrypt:) Tj
T*
(- P = C XOR keystream) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Reproduction) Tj
T*
(1. Run the challenge: python challenges/crypto-reused-iv/server.py \(listens on) Tj
T*
(http://localhost:5003\).) Tj
T*
(2. Run the exploit: python exploits/exploit_reused_iv.py.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Flag) Tj
T*
(flag{never_reuse_iv}) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Mitigation) Tj
T*
(- Never reuse a CTR nonce for the same key.) Tj
T*
(- Prefer AEAD constructions \(AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305\) with correct nonce handling.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
