(# Pwn: Off-by-One ROP) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Summary) Tj
T*
(Abuse a 1-byte overflow to corrupt the least significant byte \(LSB\) of saved RBP, then pivot) Tj
T*
(the stack into attacker-controlled data and return into win\(\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Challenge) Tj
T*
(- Bug: read\(STDIN, buf, 129\) reads up to 129 bytes into a 128-byte buffer.) Tj
T*
(- Impact: byte index 128 overwrites the first byte of saved RBP \(on x86-64, little-endian\).) Tj
T*
(- Mitigations: stack protector is disabled here \(-fno-stack-protector\) to keep focus on the) Tj
T*
(pivot.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Solution) Tj
T*
(### Why the LSB matters) Tj
T*
(If we can nudge saved RBP to point into our buffer, the function epilogue \(leave; ret\)) Tj
T*
(effectively does:) Tj
T*
(- leave: RSP = RBP; RBP = [RSP]) Tj
T*
(- ret: RIP = [RSP+8]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(So if RSP is pivoted into our buffer, we control the return target.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Steps) Tj
T*
(1. Place a fake stack frame at the start of buf:) Tj
T*
(   - fake RBP \(8 bytes\)) Tj
T*
(   - fake RIP \(8 bytes\) set to win\(\)) Tj
T*
(2. Send 129 bytes so the final byte overwrites the LSB of saved RBP and points it into buf.) Tj
T*
(3. Let the function return; execution reaches win\(\).) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
