T*
(# Pwn: Stack Canary Slip) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Summary) Tj
T*
(Leak a stack canary via a format string bug, then reuse it to bypass stack protector and) Tj
T*
(redirect execution to win\(\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Challenge) Tj
T*
(- Vulnerabilities \(in vuln\(\)\):) Tj
T*
(  - printf\(buf\) format string \(stack leak\)) Tj
T*
(  - gets\(buf\) buffer overflow) Tj
T*
(- Mitigation present: stack canary \(-fstack-protector\)) Tj
T*
(- Win condition: call win\(\) which prints flag{canary_leak_then_smash} and exits.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Solution) Tj
T*
(### High level) Tj
T*
(1. Use the format string to leak the canary value from the stack.) Tj
T*
(2. Overflow the buffer again, but include the exact canary bytes so the check passes.) Tj
T*
(3. Overwrite the saved return address with the address of win\(\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(### Notes on offsets) Tj
T*
(The correct %n$p offset for the canary is environment- and build-dependent. The intended) Tj
T*
(workflow is to fuzz offsets until you see a value that ends with 00 \(canaries typically have a) Tj
T*
(null least significant byte\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Reproduction) Tj
T*
(This challenge is intended for Linux \(or WSL\).) Tj
T*
(1. Build: make -C challenges/pwn-stack-canary/) Tj
T*
(2. Run the exploit: python exploits/exploit_stack_canary.py) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Flag) Tj
T*
(flag{canary_leak_then_smash}) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Mitigation) Tj
T*
(- Never pass user input as a format string: use printf\("%s", buf\).) Tj
T*
(- Avoid unsafe input APIs like gets\(\): use fgets\(\) with bounds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
endstream
