(# Reversing: Flag Check) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Summary) Tj
T*
(Recover the expected flag by reversing a simple XOR-based check that compares input bytes) Tj
T*
(against static arrays.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Challenge) Tj
T*
(The checker validates:) Tj
T*
(ord\(flag[i]\) XOR KEY[i] == TARGET[i]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Both KEY and TARGET are static, so the flag can be derived offline:) Tj
T*
(flag[i] = KEY[i] XOR TARGET[i]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Solution) Tj
T*
(### Steps) Tj
T*
(1. Extract KEY and TARGET from the binary \(or, in this repo, from flag_check.py\).) Tj
T*
(2. Compute each flag byte as KEY[i] XOR TARGET[i].) Tj
T*
(3. Print the reconstructed string.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Reproduction) Tj
T*
(1. Run the solver: python exploits/solve_flag_check.py) Tj
T*
(2. \(Optional\) Verify: python challenges/rev-flag-check/flag_check.py and paste the output.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Flag) Tj
T*
(flag{xor_is_not_crypto}) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(## Mitigation) Tj
T*
(- Do not embed secrets in client-side binaries if the attacker controls execution.) Tj
T*
(- Move validation and secrets server-side, or use a design that remains secure under reverse) Tj
T*
(engineering.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
